============================================================
Azure Networking Lab — Session Day 1 and 2 Log
============================================================

Repository:
    azure-networking-lab

Branch:
    feat/az-network-foundation

Location:
    centralindia

============================================================
SESSION 1 — Resource Group, VNet, Subnet, NSG
============================================================

Created Resource Group:

Command:
az group create --name az-net-lab-rg --location centralindia --tags owner=tarun

Result:
    provisioningState: Succeeded
    name: az-net-lab-rg
    location: centralindia

Observed tags:
    AutoDelete: Yes
    owner: tarun
    CreatedOn: <Azure-generated timestamp>

------------------------------------------------------------
Resource Group inspection
------------------------------------------------------------

Command:
az group show -n az-net-lab-rg -o table

Result:
    Location      Name
    centralindia  az-net-lab-rg

Note:
    Default table output does not display tags.

Attempted JMESPath query:

az group show -n az-net-lab-rg \
    --query "{Name:name, Location:location, Tags;tags}" \
    -o table

Result:
    Invalid JMESPath expression.

Cause:
    Used ';' instead of ':' in the object projection.

Correct query:

az group show -n az-net-lab-rg \
    --query "{Name:name, Location:location, Tags:tags}" \
    -o json

Result:
    Tags were displayed correctly.

More focused query:

az group show -n az-net-lab-rg \
    --query "{Name:name, Location:location, Owner:tags.owner, Autodelete:tags.AutoDelete}" \
    -o table

Result:
    Name           Location      Owner    Autodelete
    az-net-lab-rg  centralindia  tarun    Yes

Learning:
    JMESPath object keys on the left side are user-defined aliases.
    The value on the right side references the Azure JSON property.

------------------------------------------------------------
Created VNet
------------------------------------------------------------

Command:

az network vnet create \
    --resource-group az-net-lab-rg \
    -n az-net-lab-vnet \
    --address-prefix 10.0.0.0/16 \
    --location centralindia \
    --tags owner=tarun

Result:
    provisioningState: Succeeded
    name: az-net-lab-vnet
    address space: 10.0.0.0/16
    location: centralindia

------------------------------------------------------------
Created Subnet
------------------------------------------------------------

Initial attempt:

az network vnet subnet create \
    --resource-group az-net-lab-rg \
    --vnet-name az-net-lab-vnet \
    -n az-net-lab-subnet \
    --address-prefixes 10.1.0.0/24

Result:
    Failed.

Error:
    Subnet range is outside the VNet address range.

Reason:
    VNet address space is 10.0.0.0/16.
    10.1.0.0/24 is outside that address space.

Corrected command:

az network vnet subnet create \
    --resource-group az-net-lab-rg \
    --vnet-name az-net-lab-vnet \
    -n az-net-lab-subnet \
    --address-prefixes 10.0.1.0/24

Result:
    provisioningState: Succeeded

------------------------------------------------------------
Subnet inspection
------------------------------------------------------------

Command:

az network vnet show \
    --resource-group az-net-lab-rg \
    --name az-net-lab-vnet \
    --query "{Name:name, AddressSpace:addressSpace.addressPrefixes, Subnets:subnets[].{Name:name, Prefix:addressPrefix}}" \
    -o json

Result:

    {
      "AddressSpace": [
        "10.0.0.0/16"
      ],
      "Name": "az-net-lab-vnet",
      "Subnets": [
        {
          "Name": "az-net-lab-subnet",
          "Prefix": "10.0.1.0/24"
        }
      ]
    }

------------------------------------------------------------
NSG creation
------------------------------------------------------------

Created NSG:

az network nsg create \
    --resource-group az-net-lab-rg \
    --name az-net-lab-nsg \
    --location centralindia

Result:
    provisioningState: Succeeded

Observed default security rules.

Inbound defaults:
    AllowVnetInBound               65000   Allow
    AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound  65001   Allow
    DenyAllInBound                 65500   Deny

Outbound defaults:
    AllowVnetOutBound              65000   Allow
    AllowInternetOutBound          65001   Allow
    DenyAllOutBound                65500   Deny

Important:
    securityRules is empty initially.
    defaultSecurityRules contains Azure's built-in rules.

------------------------------------------------------------
Custom NSG rule
------------------------------------------------------------

Created:

Allow-HTTPS-In

Priority:
    100

Direction:
    Inbound

Access:
    Allow

Protocol:
    TCP

Source:
    Internet

Destination port:
    443

Verified with:

az network nsg rule list \
    --resource-group az-net-lab-rg \
    --nsg-name az-net-lab-nsg \
    -o table

------------------------------------------------------------
NSG association
------------------------------------------------------------

Initially:
    subnet had no NSG association.

Associated NSG with subnet:

az network vnet subnet update \
    -g az-net-lab-rg \
    --vnet-name az-net-lab-vnet \
    -n az-net-lab-subnet \
    --network-security-group az-net-lab-nsg

Result:
    NSG association succeeded.

------------------------------------------------------------
Important CLI observations
------------------------------------------------------------

Incorrect:

az network vnet create subnet ...

Correct:

az network vnet subnet create ...

Incorrect:

az network subnet show ...

Correct:

az network vnet subnet show ...

The Azure CLI command hierarchy matters.

